// OpenCode 2 flow pack — additions for ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json
// Merge these keys into the existing config (do not replace the file).
//
// Verified against opencode 2.0.9 on 2026-09-20 with `opencode debug config`,
// which prints the config AFTER normalization — the way to check that a key
// landed the way you meant it.
//
// ── One permission shape, never two ─────────────────────────────────────────
// V2 renamed `permission` (object, grouped by tool) to `permissions` (ordered
// array) and renamed two actions: `bash` -> `shell`, `task` -> `subagent`. It
// still READS the V1 object and normalizes it — measured: the block below comes
// back out of `debug config` as a `permissions` array with the order preserved.
//
// The V1 shape is what this pack writes, on purpose: `permission-config.json`
// is merged by jq, and an additive merge into an ORDERED array whose semantics
// are last-match-wins is materially harder to get right than the object merge.
// What you must not do is mix them — a `permission` object AND a `permissions`
// array in the same file set the same canonical value, and the native V2 form
// wins, silently dropping the other. Pick one; this pack picked V1-normalized.
{
  // Session sharing. opencode can upload full session history to its servers
  // (opncd.ai/s/<id>). Client code must never leave the machine by default —
  // disable it globally. /share can still opt in per-session.
  "share": "disabled",

  // Phase skills are user-invoked gates. opencode honors neither Claude's
  // `disable-model-invocation` nor anything else in SKILL.md frontmatter for
  // gating — what it reads is `metadata.opencode/autoinvoke`, and the pack's
  // skills do not carry it. So the gate is a PERMISSION: "ask" lets the model
  // propose a flow skill but requires your approval; the /flow-* commands
  // remain the normal entry point. Research stays automatic via the exception.
  "permission": {
    "skill": {
      "flow-*": "ask",
      "flow-research": "allow"
    },
    // The git/destructive-op safety floor that git-workflow.md states as prose,
    // here a hard gate. Both V1 and V2 evaluate these as glob matches where the
    // LAST matching rule wins, so the catch-all "*" goes FIRST and the specific
    // destructive patterns override it to "ask". Order is load-bearing in both
    // versions; normalization preserves it.
    "bash": {
      "*": "allow",
      "git push --force*": "ask",
      "git push -f*": "ask",
      "git reset --hard*": "ask",
      "git rebase*": "ask",
      "rm -rf*": "ask"
    }
  },
  // Optional: make the shared rubrics ambient (they are small). The flow
  // skills already reference them by path, so this is reinforcement, not a
  // requirement.
  // "instructions": ["~/.agents/skills/flow-core/references/harness-mechanics.md"],

  // Linear MCP (required by /flow-build and the specs layer). Example shape — adjust to
  // your actual server; restrict to the agents that need it if desired:
  // "mcp": { "linear": { "type": "remote", "url": "https://mcp.linear.app/mcp" } }
}

// ── Skill discovery ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// No shell env is needed. OpenCode 2 scans ~/.config/opencode/skills,
// ~/.claude/skills and ~/.agents/skills globally, plus .opencode/skills,
// .claude/skills and .agents/skills from cwd up to the project root
// (https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/skills/, read 2026-09-20).
//
// There is no longer a command to CHECK that resolution: `opencode debug skill`
// existed in V1 and is gone in V2, whose debug surface is only `agents`,
// `config` and `paths`. The V1 measurement this file used to cite (2026-08-21:
// 39 skills, 0 duplicate names) is therefore not reproducible on V2 — treat
// skill resolution as documented-but-unverified until a V2 probe exists.
// `opencode debug agents` DOES still verify the agents tree end to end.

// ── Plugins ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// V1 plugins do not run on V2: it requires `export default {id, setup}` and
// rejects the V1 named-export shape with
//   PluginModule.LoadError: Plugin must export a default definition ...
// Files in ~/.config/opencode/plugins/ are auto-discovered, so a leftover V1
// plugin is loaded, refused, and logged as a WARN on every boot — visible with
// `opencode run --print-logs`. Delete the ones you no longer want.
// flow-session-context.ts ships as a V2 plugin. /deploy-global writes it UNLESS
// it positively detects a non-V2 opencode — so: opencode 2 -> written; opencode 1
// -> skipped; a version it cannot read -> skipped; no opencode on PATH -> written,
// because the file is inert until one exists and V2 is the current major.
//
// `opencode plugin list` is how to see what actually loaded: it lists local file
// plugins with their path, and the ID column is the signal — populated for a
// plugin that loaded, "-" for one that was refused (measured 2.0.9, 2026-09-20).
// Its scope varies by working directory in a way we have not characterized: from
// one directory it listed the four global plugins, from another only a package
// one. Cross-check with `opencode run --print-logs` when it matters.
